1
00:00:00,445 --> 00:00:07,553
Bartering is how most of what the yard wants actually arrives. This tab plans a whole day of it.

2
00:00:14,375 --> 00:00:19,648
A run is for one of two things, and it asks which before anything else.

3
00:00:22,442 --> 00:00:29,593
A material: the ladder up to one thing you are short of, counted against what is already aboard.

4
00:00:33,828 --> 00:00:42,301
Or silver: every chain today's board allows, and a run along the ones you tick. That is the one to follow here.

5
00:00:44,906 --> 00:00:51,526
Tell it your barter level, because that is what every Parley figure after this is priced at.

6
00:00:51,526 --> 00:00:56,652
Now the board. Every refresh, the whole sea shows one of forty layouts.

7
00:00:56,652 --> 00:01:04,507
It cannot know which. So it asks about a single island — the one whose offer tells the forty apart best.

8
00:01:07,409 --> 00:01:16,032
The answer is on the game's own barter list, set from Normal to Level 1: find that island's row, and read across.

9
00:01:16,645 --> 00:01:18,996
Tap the same thing here.

10
00:01:22,244 --> 00:01:29,992
One look usually names the layout. Where two of the forty still fit, it says so, and asks for one more.

11
00:01:41,456 --> 00:01:49,025
And the whole board follows from that one answer: every chain the day allows, and what each of them pays.

12
00:01:49,025 --> 00:01:51,930
Next, what you want out of the day.

13
00:01:58,113 --> 00:02:07,683
Cash out today is the most silver at the wharf tonight. Build the stocks finishes every island and keeps a floor of each level back for tomorrow.

14
00:02:07,683 --> 00:02:19,954
Watch the row along the bottom as it does: that is what the preset keeps back at each level — never sold, never spent below it, so tomorrow's board has something to start from.

15
00:02:22,756 --> 00:02:30,525
Pace is the real choice. Fast and light, full but never slower than full speed, or full and loaded —

16
00:02:32,595 --> 00:02:40,343
a quarter over the limit, sailing slower for it, and calling at a wharf only where the next island would not deal.

17
00:02:43,067 --> 00:02:54,634
Quests on the way: the dailies and weeklies you have already taken, handed in where the run passes their taker — or with a stop put in a short way off the route.

18
00:02:58,072 --> 00:03:06,213
The way round: nearest islands first whatever chain they belong to, or each chain climbed to its top before the next.

19
00:03:06,213 --> 00:03:11,919
Where you sail from, where your storage is, and how long you are willing to be out.

20
00:03:11,919 --> 00:03:17,022
Then the chains on offer, and three runs already worked out for you:

21
00:03:17,047 --> 00:03:24,006
the most silver, the most an hour, and the most a Parley unit. They are rarely the same run.

22
00:03:24,132 --> 00:03:26,845
Take one of them as it stands,

23
00:03:30,908 --> 00:03:33,835
tick the chains you want yourself,

24
00:03:36,051 --> 00:03:40,471
or keep what is ticked and let it fill the rest around them.

25
00:03:44,628 --> 00:03:51,971
The bar along the foot keeps the running total: chains, silver net, hours, islands, wharf calls.

26
00:03:52,937 --> 00:03:56,802
Lay it out, and every stop of it is written down.

27
00:04:02,892 --> 00:04:06,712
What you hand over at each island, and what comes back.

28
00:04:06,712 --> 00:04:14,053
The hold after every trade, against the limit — and where it says past the limit, that is you sailing slower for it.

29
00:04:14,053 --> 00:04:19,869
Where it says too heavy to barter, the next island will not deal until you lighten first.

30
00:04:19,869 --> 00:04:26,374
Which is what a wharf call is for: the surplus goes into storage, and the run carries on.

31
00:04:26,374 --> 00:04:31,876
And the Parley bar, falling toward whatever you have left of the day's million.

32
00:04:31,876 --> 00:04:37,427
If it all looks right, sail it. The run goes onto the chart as a checklist.

33
00:04:51,202 --> 00:04:55,153
Full screen, when you would rather have the sea than the sheet.

34
00:04:55,167 --> 00:05:00,888
It follows you leg by leg, and every stop is a card with the trade on it.

35
00:05:11,390 --> 00:05:17,986
Tick each one off as you make it. What an island actually paid re-counts everything after it.

36
00:05:24,225 --> 00:05:29,925
At the end — or all at once, for a run sailed faster than it can be filmed —

37
00:05:49,473 --> 00:05:51,760
you record the trip.

38
00:05:54,838 --> 00:06:02,669
The silver goes to the purse, the goods left over into the Inventory, the quests handed in with their rewards.

39
00:06:02,669 --> 00:06:06,300
All of it as one change, and one Undo.

40
00:06:09,826 --> 00:06:16,317
And none of these figures are generic. The hold, the speed and every minute on that route are your ship:

41
00:06:16,317 --> 00:06:21,068
its hull, the four parts fitted, the crystal and the crew that is aboard.

42
00:06:21,068 --> 00:06:24,720
One island answered, and the day is planned.
